**Problem:** GSAP writes its own `transform` on the element, which overwrites the CSS `translate(-50%,-50%)`. The centering is lost the moment the tween starts, and `lint` flags it as `gsap_css_transform_conflict`.

**Fix (preferred): center with flex/inset, leave `transform` to GSAP.**

```html
<div class="title-wrap">
  <h1 id="title">Heading</h1>
</div>
```

```css
.title-wrap {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
}
#title { /* no transform here */ }
```

```js
const tl = gsap.timeline({ paused: true });
tl.fromTo("#title", { y: 40, opacity: 0 }, { y: 0, opacity: 1, duration: 0.6 }, 0);
window.__timelines["<root-composition-id>"] = tl;
```

The wrapper handles centering, so GSAP's `x`/`y` on the heading only ever means an offset from the centered position. Using `fromTo` sets the start state inside the tween rather than in CSS, which keeps seeking deterministic.

**Alternative: keep the original element and let GSAP own the centering.**

```css
#title { position: absolute; left: 50%; top: 50%; } /* no CSS transform */
```

```js
tl.fromTo("#title",
  { xPercent: -50, yPercent: -50, y: 40, opacity: 0 },
  { xPercent: -50, yPercent: -50, y: 0, opacity: 1, duration: 0.6 }, 0);
```

`xPercent`/`yPercent` are folded into the same GSAP transform as `x`/`y`, so nothing conflicts.

**Notes:**
- Don't put the wrapper's centering on a node GSAP also transforms. Keep them on separate elements, as above.
- The timeline must stay paused and be registered under the key matching the root `data-composition-id`.
- Don't add a scene-exit `visibility: hidden` set. The runtime handles clip visibility.
- Afterward, run `npx hyperframes check`. A passing `gsap_css_transform_conflict` rule confirms the fix.

placebo.answer:
**Cause:** GSAP writes its x/y animation to the element's inline `transform` (as `translate(x, y)`), which overwrites the CSS `transform: translate(-50%, -50%)`. The centering is lost the moment the tween starts, so the heading jumps.

**Fix: let GSAP own the centering.** Remove the CSS transform and use `xPercent`/`yPercent`, which GSAP composes with `x`/`y` in a single transform matrix.

```css
.heading {
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%;
  left: 50%;
  /* no transform here */
}
```

```js
// Set centering once, before any tween
gsap.set(".heading", { xPercent: -50, yPercent: -50 });

// Animate x/y freely; the -50% offsets are preserved
gsap.to(".heading", { x: 100, y: 40, duration: 1, ease: "power2.out" });
```

Or, in one call with `fromTo`:

```js
gsap.fromTo(".heading",
  { xPercent: -50, yPercent: -50, x: 0, y: 0 },
  { x: 100, y: 40, duration: 1 }
);
```

**Why this is deterministic:**
- GSAP tracks `xPercent`, `yPercent`, `x`, and `y` as separate values and builds the final transform from all of them, so nothing is clobbered between ticks.
- The starting state is explicit (`gsap.set` or the `fromTo` from-values), so it doesn't depend on parsing a CSS transform or on layout timing.
- Percent offsets are resolved against the element's own size, so the result is the same as CSS `translate(-50%, -50%)`.

**Alternatives if you must keep CSS centering:**
- Use `translate: -50% -50%` (the individual CSS property) instead of `transform`. It composes with GSAP's `transform` writes, though browser support and GSAP's handling of it should be verified for your target browsers.
- Or center with flexbox/grid on a wrapper (`display: grid; place-items: center`) and animate only `x`/`y` on the heading, so no translate-based centering is needed at all.

**Pitfalls to avoid:**
- Don't mix a CSS `transform` with GSAP transform properties on the same element.
- Avoid `gsap.set` for `xPercent`/`yPercent` after a tween has already run, since it can cause a visible jump. Set it once up front.

