**Cause:** GSAP writes the element's whole `transform` inline. When it first parses the element, it reads the computed matrix, so the CSS `translate(-50%, -50%)` gets baked into pixel values for x/y, or is overwritten. The result is that the centering offset is either lost or frozen at the pixel size from that moment. A later resize or font load then makes the heading drift or jump, and repeated or reversed tweens start from an inconsistent origin.

**Fix:** Remove the centering from the CSS `transform` and give it to GSAP as `xPercent`/`yPercent`. GSAP keeps these separate from `x`/`y`, so the percentage centering stays live and the pixel animation is applied on top of it.

```css
.heading {
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%;
  left: 50%;
  /* no transform here */
}
```

```js
gsap.set(".heading", { xPercent: -50, yPercent: -50 });

gsap.fromTo(".heading",
  { x: -100, y: 0 },
  { x: 0, y: 0, duration: 1, ease: "power2.out" }
);
```

Why this is deterministic:
- `xPercent`/`yPercent` are tracked in GSAP's transform cache, so the centering is always `-50%` of the current element size, independent of when the tween starts.
- Using `fromTo` with explicit start values means the animation doesn't depend on whatever the transform happened to be when it was parsed.
- Nothing in the CSS competes with GSAP for `transform`.

**Alternative:** If you'd rather keep centering in CSS, put it on a wrapper and animate the heading inside it.

```html
<div class="center"><h1 class="heading">Title</h1></div>
```

```css
.center { position: absolute; top: 50%; left: 50%; transform: translate(-50%, -50%); }
```

GSAP then animates only `.heading`, so the two transforms never interact. Use the `xPercent`/`yPercent` approach if you want to avoid the extra element.

